<mxCell id="interview" value="INTERVIEW KEY QUESTIONS: Q1: How achieve less than 2 second video startup? A1: FFmpeg pipeline transcodes 4 resolutions into HLS 6-second segments, CloudFront CDN 200+ edges routes to nearest under 50ms with 95% cache hit, adaptive bitrate player measures bandwidth via first segment and selects resolution. Math: DNS 50ms + TLS 100ms + manifest 200ms + first segment 500ms equals 850ms total. Q2: How track progress for millions of concurrent students? A2: Client batches 3 heartbeats over 30 seconds reducing API call volume 3x, Kafka video.progress topic 100 partitions by user_id absorbs bursts returning 200 OK immediately without blocking playback, consumer upserts last_position_sec always latest for resume versus GREATEST(max_position_sec, new) for completion which never decreases on rewind, completion triggers at 95% watched threshold. Q3: How prevent double enrollment and double charging? A3: Five layers - client generated enrollmentRequestId deduplicates Stripe PaymentIntent on double-click, webhook signature verification prevents fake events, Kafka consumer checks DB before INSERT skipping duplicate webhook delivery, UNIQUE(user_id,course_id) constraint is the database-level safety net, polling backup queries Stripe every 5 minutes catching lost webhooks. Q4: Why two position fields in Progress table? A4: last_position_sec is always the latest value used purely to resume playback, max_position_sec never decreases via GREATEST and is used purely for completion check, this separation prevents a rewind-then-close scenario from incorrectly un-completing a students progress. Q5: Why not sync transcode on upload? A5: A 2GB video takes 10-15 minutes to transcode into 4 resolutions plus HLS packaging, holding an HTTP connection that long causes timeouts and wastes always-on compute, so instructors upload directly to S3 via presigned URL bypassing the server entirely and Spot instance workers process asynchronously at 70% lower cost. Q6: Why is Access table separate from Enrollments? A6: Enrollment is a permanent historical record that never changes, Access is a keycard with scope_type and status that can be revoked on refund or expired on subscription lapse, Video Playback Service checks Access not Enrollments before issuing a signed CloudFront URL, letting refunds work without ever touching enrollment history." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e3f2fd;strokeColor=#1976d2;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="1870" width="2480" height="20" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
</root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>
